Definition:
Memory management is a function of the operating system that handles the allocation and deallocation of memory to processes and ensures efficient utilization of main memory.

Objectives of Memory Management:

Efficient memory utilization

Process isolation and protection

Support for multiprogramming

Minimization of fragmentation

Faster memory access

Functions of Memory Management:

Allocation and deallocation of memory

Tracking memory usage

Address translation (logical to physical address)

Memory protection

Sharing memory among processes

Types of Memory Allocation

1. Contiguous Allocation
A process is allocated a single continuous block of memory.
Problems: external fragmentation.

2. Non-contiguous Allocation
A process is divided into parts and stored in different memory locations.
Examples: paging and segmentation.

Paging:
Paging divides memory into fixed-size blocks called pages and frames.

Eliminates external fragmentation

May cause internal fragmentation

Segmentation:
Segmentation divides memory into logical segments such as code, data, and stack.

Supports logical organization

May cause external fragmentation

Virtual Memory:
Virtual memory allows execution of processes even if they are not completely in main memory by using secondary storage.

Advantages:

Large program execution

Improved multiprogramming

Efficient memory utilization

Fragmentation Types

Internal Fragmentation:
Unused memory inside allocated block.

External Fragmentation:
Free memory available but scattered in small blocks.

Advantages of Memory Management:

Efficient memory usage

Process protection

Increased system performance

Supports multitasking

